ZERO BROWSER NAVIGATION, EVENTS AND FRAME CHECKS
Date: 2026-11-18

Result: 76 executable original Zero cases passed; checker exit status0.
Product source: browser/navigation-check.zero.
Compiler: original Zero0.13 desktop-hooks candidate, Linux x64 host output.
The resulting executable is79839 bytes. Fixture data and assertions are Zero;
no browser-engine or third-party source was copied into the project.

Scope
The checker composes browser/address.zero.part, ui.zero.part and
render.zero.part into native executable code, with fake caller-owned memory
and injected input events. It also composes parse.zero.part unchanged, but
does not execute the separate HTML-reader application; that is tested by its
own checks and the actual VM. These tests do not simulate successful TCP.

Coverage
Bare hosts and ports; absolute, relative and root-relative links; scheme-
relative links; query replacement and fragment-only navigation; colons in
query/fragment; case-insensitive HTTP; literal percent escapes; dot and parent
path segments; trailing dot/parent directories; bare host/port query handling.
Unsafe schemes, HTTPS, backslashes, spaces, CRLF, empty authority, empty input,
256-byte input and normalized-address overflow are rejected. A255-byte final
URL succeeds. Parent segments cannot climb above the URL authority.

Real UI branches cover back, reload, history underflow, full-history rollover,
new navigation after going back, link selection/wrap/follow, unsafe links,
Up/Down scrolling, opening the address field while loading, keyboard input
limit, empty/ordinary Backspace, Enter and Escape during an active request.
Root-relative redirects, blocked HTTPS redirects, five-redirect limit,
missing Location, compressed-response errors, unsupported MIME and stale
request generations are checked. No successful page execution is fabricated.

Frame checks cover the maximum32768-byte document, all32 link slots, the end
of the document, the input caret,127-byte errors and control/high-byte output
sanitization. Canary bytes surround the65536-byte session,1296-byte frame,
256-byte normalized-address scratch and256-byte network request. Hashes check
that original fixture input, all link records and Location input are unchanged.
Back/reload preserve the complete history byte-for-byte. Reload preserves an
input URL that aliases the current URL buffer. A separate shell-input sentinel
remains unchanged for every case, including command-like page/address bytes.
These are deterministic bounded checks, not a formal proof of memory safety.

Regressions fixed in address.zero.part
The first54-case run exposed11 failures: fragment-only links lost the base
query; query/fragment colons were mistaken for schemes; dot segments remained
unresolved; uppercase HTTP failed; and an authority followed by a query lacked
the required slash. The implementation now normalizes these within255 bytes.
Subsequent port/query and port/fragment cases were added and passed. The source
URL/link is read-only; normalized output is completed in private scratch
before publishing a new request generation.

